## Sprint 3.4: Negative Index Guard Test
## CRITICAL SAFETY TEST: Verifies that negative array indices are rejected
##
## Physical Reality Rule: Hardware indices cannot be negative.
## In software, Array[-1] might mean "last element."
## In hardware, Bus[-1] is a physical impossibility—an atom that doesn't exist.
##
## EXPECTED BEHAVIOR: Compiler must throw P44 error (Negative Array Index)
## MUST NOT: Create nets with negative indices or crash

import * from @std/primitives/units
import Silicon_P from @std/materials/doped_semiconductors
import Copper from @std/materials/conductors

## ============================================================================
## SPACE: Negative Index Test (Should Fail with P44 Error)
## ============================================================================

space NegativeIndexTest:
    dimensions: 100mm by 20mm by 2mm
    grid: 100000 by 20000 by 2
    origin: tl by t
    
    ## Base substrate
    add substrate(Silicon_P) spanning [x:1um, y:1um, z:0] to [x:99999um, y:19999um, z:1]
    
    ## ========================================================================
    ## TEST: Negative Index (i - 5)
    ## Loop: i in 0..3 → [0, 1, 2, 3]
    ## Expression: i - 5
    ## Result: -5, -4, -3, -2 (ALL NEGATIVE - PHYSICAL IMPOSSIBILITY)
    ## 
    ## EXPECTED: P44 error with message like:
    ##   "Net index cannot be negative: D[-5]"
    ##   "Physical Reality: Hardware indices must be >= 0"
    ## ========================================================================
    for i in 0..3:
        add pour(Copper) named Test_i on z:1:
            net: D[i - 5]
            boundary: [x: i * 10mm + 1mm, y: 2mm] to [x: i * 10mm + 8mm, y: 4mm]
